---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = 'Inclusive programme — Macbett';
const description = 'An accessible guide to prepare for Tan Tan Teatro’s production of Macbett.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} locale="en">
  <main class="inclusive-booklet-page" id="main-content">
    <p class="inclusive-booklet-page__back">
      <a href="/spettacoli/en/macbett-ionesco/#materials">&larr; Back to Macbett</a>
    </p>

    <header class="inclusive-booklet-hero">
      <p class="inclusive-booklet-hero__kicker">Inclusive programme</p>
      <h1>Macbett</h1>
      <p class="inclusive-booklet-hero__subtitle">Eugène Ionesco, 1972</p>
      <p class="inclusive-booklet-hero__lede">
        An accessible guide to the performance: plot, characters, key story moments,
        stage space, sensory information and useful material before, during and after the show.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="inclusive-booklet-tools" aria-label="Accessibility tools">
      <a href="#plot"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span><span>Plot</span></a>
      <a href="#key-moments"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span><span>Key moments</span></a>
      <a href="#characters"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span><span>Characters</span></a>
      <a href="#available-accessibility"><span aria-hidden="true">04</span><span>Accessibility</span></a>
    </section>

    <figure class="inclusive-booklet-image">
      <img src="/img/macbett01.jpg" alt="Stefano Martelli as Macbett, on stage in a dark costume with a tense expression." />
    </figure>

    <div class="inclusive-booklet-layout">
      <article class="inclusive-booklet-main">
        <section id="show-at-a-glance">
          <h2>The show at a glance</h2>
          <p>
            <em>Macbett</em> is based on Eugène Ionesco’s 1972 play. The work rewrites
            Shakespeare’s <em>Macbeth</em> in a grotesque key: at its centre are the hunger for power,
            political violence, the absurdity of war and the way public language can lose meaning.
          </p>
          <p>
            Tan Tan Teatro’s version is essential and focused on the actors’ play, the rhythm of speech
            and a sound world made of music, noises, marches, repetitions and sudden shifts in energy.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="what-to-expect">
          <h2>What to expect</h2>
          <dl class="inclusive-booklet-facts">
            <div><dt>Running time</dt><dd>About 1 hour.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Interval</dt><dd>No interval.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Structure</dt><dd>A continuous sequence of scenes and transformations, without a pause.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Music and sound</dt><dd>The show includes music, noises, fanfares, marches, choral voices and intense sound moments.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Lighting</dt><dd>The stage uses contrast, half-light, darkness and changes of atmosphere.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Audience contact</dt><dd>No physical contact with the audience is planned.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Sensitive themes</dt><dd>War, betrayal, murder, power, manipulation, political violence, death and madness.</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section id="plot">
          <h2>Plot</h2>
          <p>
            Macbett and Banco are two generals serving King Duncan. After an internal war,
            they crush a rebellion and appear to become indispensable to the kingdom. Power,
            however, does not bring stability: it opens new desires, suspicions, promises and rivalries.
          </p>
          <p>
            Around Macbett, a grotesque machine of commands, ceremonies, flattery and deception
            begins to take shape. The king appears solemn but ridiculous; the winners become new
            claimants; words of honour become tools of domination. Macbett is pushed towards murder
            and towards the seizure of the throne.
          </p>
          <p>
            When Macbett reaches power, the world does not become more orderly. Violence repeats
            itself, the dead return as intrusive presences, and the ruler increasingly resembles the one
            he has removed. The story shows a cycle of power that changes faces but not its logic.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="key-moments">
          <h2>Key story moments</h2>
          <div class="inclusive-booklet-act">
            <p class="inclusive-booklet-act__label">First moment</p>
            <h3>War and reward</h3>
            <p>
              The show opens in a world of war, orders and hierarchy. Macbett and Banco fight for Duncan
              and help restore control over the kingdom. Victory is celebrated, but ambition, calculation
              and instability are already growing beneath the surface.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="inclusive-booklet-act">
            <p class="inclusive-booklet-act__label">Second moment</p>
            <h3>The promise of power</h3>
            <p>
              Macbett receives signs, temptations and words that make him imagine a higher destiny.
              Power appears as something one can take, not only receive. The performance often works
              through repetition and distortion: the characters sound solemn, but their words become
              excessive, ridiculous or threatening.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="inclusive-booklet-act">
            <p class="inclusive-booklet-act__label">Third moment</p>
            <h3>Murder and transformation</h3>
            <p>
              Macbett is driven towards the elimination of Duncan. The political act also becomes a
              theatrical act: the court changes master, but keeps the same violence. Whoever takes the
              throne must immediately defend it, and every alliance can become a threat.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="inclusive-booklet-act">
            <p class="inclusive-booklet-act__label">Fourth moment</p>
            <h3>The cycle continues</h3>
            <p>
              The final part shows a power that cannot create a real order. The ghosts of the past,
              fear and the absurdity of commands make the new ruler fragile. The fall of one tyrant is
              not enough: another order may be born through the same mechanisms.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="characters">
          <h2>Characters</h2>
          <dl class="inclusive-booklet-characters">
            <div><dt>Macbett</dt><dd>A brave and ambitious general. At first he serves the king; then he is drawn by the possibility of taking the throne.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Banco</dt><dd>A general and Macbett’s companion. He is an ally, a witness and a possible obstacle in the race for power.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Duncan</dt><dd>The king of the country. He is a figure of authority, but also a grotesque character exposed to fear, vanity and political violence.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Lady Duncan / Lady Macbett</dt><dd>A central female figure in the transformation of power. She seduces, directs and pushes the action towards crime and conquest.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Candor and Glamiss</dt><dd>Rebel lords and then titles of power. Their names mark the passage from war to a new ambition.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Macol</dt><dd>A figure linked to succession and to the possibility of a new political order, not necessarily a more just one.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Officers, soldiers, ladies, bishop, lemonade seller, apparitions and chorus</dt><dd>Figures that compose the grotesque world of the show: army, court, ritual, trade, prophecy and crowd.</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section id="stage-space">
          <h2>The stage space</h2>
          <figure class="inclusive-booklet-inline-image">
            <img src="/img/macbett02.jpg" alt="A choral scene from Macbett, with several performers arranged on a dark stage." />
          </figure>
          <p>
            The stage space is essential. The set does not realistically reconstruct castles,
            battlefields or throne rooms: it suggests places of power through bodies, sounds,
            positions, entrances and exits.
          </p>
          <p>
            The performers often build collective images: military formations, ceremonies,
            apparitions, small processions and moments of stillness. The tone can change quickly:
            a solemn scene may become comic, threatening or absurd.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="sensory-information">
          <h2>Sensory information</h2>
          <ul class="inclusive-booklet-list">
            <li>The show includes shouting, orders, verbal repetition and moments of strong vocal intensity.</li>
            <li>Sound may include marches, fanfares, blows, sudden noises and live or recorded music.</li>
            <li>The stage may alternate between darkness, half-light and strong visual contrasts.</li>
            <li>Some scenes evoke war, murder, threat, death and apparitions.</li>
            <li>The tone is grotesque: violent events may be treated with black or absurd comedy.</li>
            <li>The language may feel insistent, distorted or deliberately excessive.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="before-during-after">
          <h2>Before, during and after the show</h2>
          <p>
            Before the performance, you can read the plot, the key story moments and the sensory information.
            During the performance, you can follow the general movement without catching every single line:
            many scenes work through rhythm, accumulation, contrast and repetition.
          </p>
          <p>
            After the show, you can return to this page to reconstruct the sequence of events and identify
            the main passages: war, temptation, crime, coronation, new fall. For specific requests about
            surtitles, audio description or accessible materials, you can write to
            <a href="mailto:accessibilita@tantanteatro.it">accessibilita@tantanteatro.it</a>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="available-accessibility">
          <h2>Available accessibility</h2>
          <p>
            Surtitles for this performance are available in Italian, English and French via screen projection,
            backdrop projection or a dedicated platform. Audio descriptions are available in Italian and are
            being developed in other languages or on request.
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>

      <aside class="inclusive-booklet-toc" aria-label="Programme index">
        <h2>Index</h2>
        <a href="#show-at-a-glance">The show at a glance</a>
        <a href="#what-to-expect">What to expect</a>
        <a href="#plot">Plot</a>
        <a href="#key-moments">Key story moments</a>
        <a href="#characters">Characters</a>
        <a href="#stage-space">The stage space</a>
        <a href="#sensory-information">Sensory information</a>
        <a href="#before-during-after">Before, during and after</a>
        <a href="#available-accessibility">Available accessibility</a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </main>

  <style>
    .inclusive-booklet-page { width: min(1120px, 100%); margin: 0 auto; padding: 3vw 0 1vw; font-size: 1.9rem; line-height: 1.6; }
    .inclusive-booklet-page h1, .inclusive-booklet-page h2, .inclusive-booklet-page h3, .inclusive-booklet-page p, .inclusive-booklet-page dt, .inclusive-booklet-page dd, .inclusive-booklet-page li { color: var(--color-base); }
    .inclusive-booklet-page__back { margin: 0 0 2rem; font-family: var(--font-sans-serif); font-size: 1.35rem; }
    .inclusive-booklet-page__back a { color: var(--color-base); text-decoration: none; opacity: 0.72; }
    .inclusive-booklet-page__back a:hover, .inclusive-booklet-page__back a:focus { opacity: 1; text-decoration: none; }
    .inclusive-booklet-hero { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1rem; padding: 7vw 0 4vw; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .inclusive-booklet-hero__kicker, .inclusive-booklet-hero__subtitle { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-sans-serif); font-size: 1.3rem; font-variant: small-caps; letter-spacing: 0.16em; text-transform: lowercase; opacity: 0.62; }
    .inclusive-booklet-hero h1 { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(5rem, 10vw, 12rem); font-weight: var(--font-normal); line-height: 0.95; letter-spacing: -0.04em; }
    .inclusive-booklet-hero__lede { max-width: 860px; margin: 2rem 0 0; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 4vw, 4.8rem); line-height: 1.05; letter-spacing: -0.03em; }
    .inclusive-booklet-tools { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); margin: 3rem 0 5rem; padding: 2.2rem; color: #fff; background: #111; border-radius: 10px; }
    .inclusive-booklet-tools a { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 1rem; min-height: 5.4rem; padding: 0 1.5rem; color: #fff; border-right: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.28); font-family: var(--font-sans-serif); font-size: 1.45rem; line-height: 1.2; text-decoration: none; }
    .inclusive-booklet-tools a:last-child { border-right: 0; }
    .inclusive-booklet-tools a:hover, .inclusive-booklet-tools a:focus { background: rgba(255,255,255,0.12); text-decoration: none; }
    .inclusive-booklet-tools span[aria-hidden='true'] { min-width: 2.2rem; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 2.4rem; }
    .inclusive-booklet-image, .inclusive-booklet-inline-image { margin: 0 0 5rem; }
    .inclusive-booklet-image img, .inclusive-booklet-inline-image img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 10px; }
    .inclusive-booklet-layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 760px) minmax(220px, 1fr); gap: 5rem; align-items: start; }
    .inclusive-booklet-main section { margin: 0 0 5.5rem; scroll-margin-top: 4rem; }
    .inclusive-booklet-main h2 { margin: 0 0 1.4rem; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(3rem, 4vw, 5rem); font-weight: var(--font-normal); line-height: 1; letter-spacing: -0.03em; }
    .inclusive-booklet-main h3 { margin: 0 0 1rem; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 2.4rem; line-height: 1.15; }
    .inclusive-booklet-main p, .inclusive-booklet-main li { margin: 0 0 1.2rem; font-size: 2.05rem; line-height: 1.62; }
    .inclusive-booklet-facts { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 0; margin: 2rem 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .inclusive-booklet-facts div { padding: 1.8rem 1.8rem 1.8rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .inclusive-booklet-facts dt { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; font-family: var(--font-sans-serif); font-size: 1.2rem; font-variant: small-caps; letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: lowercase; opacity: 0.64; }
    .inclusive-booklet-facts dd { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 2rem; line-height: 1.28; }
    .inclusive-booklet-act { padding: 2.2rem 0 2.4rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .inclusive-booklet-act:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .inclusive-booklet-act__label { margin: 0 0 0.7rem !important; font-family: var(--font-sans-serif); font-size: 1.25rem !important; font-variant: small-caps; letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: lowercase; opacity: 0.62; }
    .inclusive-booklet-characters { margin: 2rem 0 0; }
    .inclusive-booklet-characters div { padding: 1.4rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .inclusive-booklet-characters div:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .inclusive-booklet-characters dt { margin-bottom: 0.4rem; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 2.35rem; line-height: 1.2; }
    .inclusive-booklet-characters dd { margin: 0; font-size: 1.8rem; line-height: 1.5; opacity: 0.82; }
    .inclusive-booklet-list { padding-left: 1.4em; }
    .inclusive-booklet-toc { position: sticky; top: 3rem; padding-left: 2rem; border-left: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .inclusive-booklet-toc h2 { margin: 0 0 1.2rem; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 2.2rem; font-weight: var(--font-normal); }
    .inclusive-booklet-toc a { display: block; margin: 0 0 0.8rem; color: var(--color-base); font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.45rem; line-height: 1.35; text-decoration: none; opacity: 0.72; }
    .inclusive-booklet-toc a:hover, .inclusive-booklet-toc a:focus { opacity: 1; text-decoration: none; }
    [data-theme='dark'] .inclusive-booklet-tools { background: #050505; }
    [data-theme='dark'] .inclusive-booklet-page__back a, [data-theme='dark'] .inclusive-booklet-toc a { color: #f5f5f5; }
    @media (max-width: 980px) { .inclusive-booklet-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .inclusive-booklet-toc { position: static; padding-left: 0; border-left: 0; } .inclusive-booklet-tools { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); } .inclusive-booklet-tools a:nth-child(2) { border-right: 0; } }
    @media (max-width: 640px) { .inclusive-booklet-page { font-size: 1.75rem; } .inclusive-booklet-tools, .inclusive-booklet-facts { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .inclusive-booklet-tools { padding: 1.4rem; } .inclusive-booklet-tools a { padding: 1.25rem 0; border-right: 0; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.28); } .inclusive-booklet-tools a:last-child { border-bottom: 0; } .inclusive-booklet-main p, .inclusive-booklet-main li, .inclusive-booklet-facts dd { font-size: 1.9rem; } }
  </style>
</Layout>